    suitability for any purpose.

Pinned revisions
----------------

Unless listed below, files are from sublimehq/Packages commit
e4e988e202322a04bea88e6d991f3dc537600731 (2026-07-19).

syntect 5.x cannot load `version: 2` grammars that use `extends:`, so these
three are pinned to their last `version: 1` revisions instead:

  HTML.sublime-syntax   <- HTML/HTML.sublime-syntax   @ eb30451f0df3259cf1bfc219265a21ddfe9c0630 (2020-07-20)
  SQL.sublime-syntax    <- SQL/SQL.sublime-syntax     @ 4a51942b8e0792ad0818a5aa41f6d95cfea9f2da (2024-07-13)
  Diff.sublime-syntax   <- Diff/Diff.sublime-syntax   @ c275309827fc9551873975e92b9a4b2d0b183b32 (2024-02-18)

File mapping (upstream path -> local name):

  Rust/Rust.sublime-syntax                                -> Rust.sublime-syntax
  JavaScript/JavaScript.sublime-syntax                    -> JavaScript.sublime-syntax
  JavaScript/Regular Expressions (JavaScript).sublime-syntax -> JavaScript-RegExp.sublime-syntax
  Python/Python.sublime-syntax                            -> Python.sublime-syntax
  Go/Go.sublime-syntax                                    -> Go.sublime-syntax
  C++/C.sublime-syntax                                    -> C.sublime-syntax
  C++/C++.sublime-syntax                                  -> C++.sublime-syntax
  ShellScript/Bash.sublime-syntax                         -> Bash.sublime-syntax
  ShellScript/Shell-Unix-Generic.sublime-syntax           -> Shell-Unix-Generic.sublime-syntax
  JSON/JSON.sublime-syntax                                -> JSON.sublime-syntax
  YAML/YAML.sublime-syntax                                -> YAML.sublime-syntax
  TOML/TOML.sublime-syntax                                -> TOML.sublime-syntax
  CSS/CSS.sublime-syntax                                  -> CSS.sublime-syntax
  Java/Java.sublime-syntax                                -> Java.sublime-syntax

Hand-authored files (no upstream provenance, MIT like the rest of pi):

  Plain-Text.sublime-syntax              minimal `text.plain` fallback target for
                                         syntect's unresolved-`embed` handling
  Python-RegExp.sublime-syntax           minimal `source.regexp.python` stub
  Python-RegExp-RawFString.sublime-syntax minimal `source.regexp.python.embedded.raw-f-string` stub

  The two Python regexp stubs exist because upstream ships the real ones
  (`Python/Embeddings/RegExp (for Python)*.sublime-syntax`) as `version: 2`
  grammars. Without the stubs, raw strings passed to `re.*` leave unresolved
  context references that abort highlighting for the whole block.

Known omissions
---------------

  * TypeScript/TSX: upstream's grammar has been `version: 2`/`extends:`-only
    since 2020; `ts`/`tsx` fences are highlighted with the JavaScript grammar.
  * Go backtick-string magic-language injections (`// language=css` comments)
    reference per-language `source.go.embedded-backtick-string.*` grammars we
    do not vendor; hitting one degrades that block to plain text.
